  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Post-divestment MPACT is a Singapore-anchored trust (61% of AUM, 66% of NPI) with a shrinking North Asia tail. FY27 DI to unitholders ~S$400m (DPU ~7.5-7.6c) after the full-year loss of Festival Walk Tower, TSI and ASY, partly offset by interest savings. Thereafter ~2% revenue growth from VivoCity and MBC reversions against soft HK/China/Japan offices, slight DI margin lift as debt costs ease. Capitalised at 7.0% CoE, 1.5% terminal growth.",
    "bull": "Singapore reversions stay high-single-digit, HK Festival Walk retail stabilises, further overseas divestments at or near book are recycled into debt reduction or Singapore assets, and SGD rates stay low. DI margin rises to ~51%; 6.5% CoE as the market re-rates a cleaner Singapore REIT toward NAV.",
    "bear": "Greater China and Japan office vacancies and negative reversions persist, HK retail weakens further, the capital-distribution component of DI falls away and renewed inflation lifts refinancing costs. Revenue falls 6% then 1% before low growth; DI margin ~46.5%, maintenance/AEI capex 5.5% of revenue, CoE 7.75%."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Value the distribution stream unitholders actually receive, net of the units issued to the manager and of the capex needed to keep the buildings lettable, capitalised at an SGD cost of equity. Distributable income is post-interest, so borrowings are not deducted again. NAV (S$1.73) is a cross-check, not the valuation: external-manager REITs with declining DPU rarely trade at book.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Interest-rate path: SGD/HKD refinancing costs drive the DI margin and the cost of equity simultaneously",
      "Greater China (Festival Walk retail, Gateway Plaza, Sandhill Plaza) and Japan office occupancy and negative reversions",
      "Capital distribution (S$27.3m FY26) may not recur at that level",
      "External-manager fee leakage and unit issuance for fees",
      "FX translation of HKD/JPY/RMB/KRW income against SGD",
      "Further divestments below book would crystallise NAV losses"
    ]
  },
